PROJECT TITLE: PARTICLE DYNAMIC SIMULATION

PROJECT DESCRIPTION:
	
	This project will consist of programming a simple 2 dimensional simulation of 
	collisions between a particle and a box that encloses the particle inside. This
	will introduce concepts like how to predict the future motion of a particle, how
	to handle a collision between a moving particle and a stationary bounding box, 
	and how to resolve collisions. After, we can expand the simulation to have multiple
	particles present and this will introduce a problem with particle collisions between
	two moving masses, how to resolve these collisions, and how make the simulation
	rendering more efficient. 

PROJECT PHASE #1: PARTICLE DEFINITION

	A particle is a point mass that has a position, velocity, and acceleration
	vectors. These three peices of information is going to be apart of definition
	of what a particle is. Starting out, we are going to assume that the acceleration
	acting on the particle is going to constant throughout the system, and this 
	assumption is called a constraint. A constraint is a rule imposed on the 
	simulation to limit motion of a particle. Formally, we can define a particle as
	the following, 
	
		Particle := {
						(pos_x, pos_y), 
						(vel_x, vel_y), 
						(accel_x, accel_y), 
						radius
					}.

	Note that we have added an extra feild called radius of type float because we are representing
	a particle as a circle and in raylib a circle's definition is having a center and a radius. 

PROJECT PHASE #2: TIME DEFINTION

	Before we can simulate a particle moving about in a box, we still need to define a way 
	that allows us to use some notion of time. Looking at kinematics we can see that time 
	is used to determine and keep track one single moment of a systems cycle. We can use 
	a concept called frames per second in animation as a notion of time in our simulation.
	Frames per second or FPS is a measure of how many frames/images have been rendered to
	the screen in a second. Having an FPS of 60, would mean that 60 frames/images have been
	rendered to the screen in a span of 1 second. Additionally, using the fact that computers
	work in discrete amount of time, we can take the inverse to define delta_t which will
	represent a small amount of time in the system. Below is a formal defintion of delta_t:

		delta_t = 1 / FPS.
	
	This delta_t value is going to be used to define average motion over an interval. 

PROJECT PHASE #3: INITIAL SIMULATION STATE

	At the start of the simulation, there are going to be some initial values for a particle,
	these include an initial (constant) acceleration, an initial position, and an initial 
	velocity vectors. Since you have a constant acceleration, you would then integrate that
	acceleration and get a velocity of the following, 

		v = v_0 + a * delta_t.

	This velocity equation allows the simulation to predict a future velocity value of the 
	particle based on the previous value of velocity from the previous frame. Integrating 
	the velocity equation, you will get a similar formula for position, 

		p = p_0 + v_0 * delta_t.

	Again, this position will allow us to predict a future position value of the particle based 
	one the previous values of position and velocity from the previous frame. Using these two 
	formulas we can have a discrete process of simulating the movement of a single particle that
	stems from a set of initial states of a particle. 

PROJECT PHASE #4: COLLISION DETECTION WITH A CONTAINER (BOX)

	When it comes to collision detection with a container, we will use the window borders itself to check 
	and resolve collisions for now. Checking whether a particle has collided with once of the four borders
	of the window requires you to either add or substract the radius from the center position of the particle
	and then depedning on where that distance intersects the border, you can simply flip the direction of the
	velocity vector x or y component. The reason for why to add the particle radius with the center position of
	the particle is to get a point on the border of the particle in the top, bottom, right, and left directions 
	of the particle. By using that point you can see if it intersects or goes beyond the four borders of the 
	window, and if so then change the direction of the particles velocity component. 

	Note that this method of checking and resolving collisions between the window border and the particle is 
	a discrete way of colliison detection. What I mean by discrete, is that the simulation only checks the 
	collision detection once a frame of rendering the particle to the screen and does not always check collisions
	between the borders and the particle. This makes it so that if the particle has a fast initial speed or speeds
	up as the simulation progresses, the particle can just fly of the screen beyond the borders of the screen. This
	is a very common problem in simulations and has its own name of Tunnelling. 

	The soultion to overcome tunnelling is to (a) set a speed limit of the particle, (b) increase the frames per second,
	or (c) implement a new collision detection system called continuous collision detection. 

